---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: "What is the difference between PDF and PDF/A?", a: "PDF/A is a stricter version of PDF. Everything needed to display the document, such as fonts and colour information, must be inside the file, and features like JavaScript are not allowed." },
  { q: "Which PDF/A version should I use?", a: "Use the version the court, university or office asks for. PDF/A-2b is widely accepted and is what PDFSherpa creates." },
  { q: "Can a PDF/A have a password?", a: "No. Encryption is not allowed in PDF/A, because an archive must be readable in the future without a key." },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="what-is-pdf-a" faqs={faqs}>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> PDF/A is the archive version of PDF (ISO 19005). It stores fonts and colour information inside the file so the document looks the same in decades. To convert, open <a href="/pdf-to-pdfa">PDF to PDF/A</a>, select the file and click <strong>Convert to PDF/A</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Why PDF/A exists</h2>
  <p>
    A normal PDF can depend on fonts installed on the computer that opens it, link to outside content, or contain scripts. In twenty years, those may be
    missing and the document may look different or not open. PDF/A forbids anything that depends on the outside world, so the file is self-contained.
  </p>

  <h2>Who asks for PDF/A</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Courts with electronic filing.</li>
    <li>Universities, for theses and dissertations.</li>
    <li>Government tenders and public archives.</li>
    <li>Companies that must keep invoices and contracts for many years.</li>
  </ul>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>The versions in brief</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PDF/A-1:</strong> the first version (2005), most restrictive. No transparency.</li>
    <li><strong>PDF/A-2:</strong> adds transparency, JPEG 2000 and better compression. Widely accepted.</li>
    <li><strong>PDF/A-3:</strong> like PDF/A-2, but allows attached files.</li>
    <li>The letter <strong>b</strong> (basic) means the document will look the same. <strong>a</strong> (accessible) adds tagged structure for screen readers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to convert</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/pdf-to-pdfa">pdfsherpa.com/pdf-to-pdfa</a> and select the PDF.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Convert to PDF/A</strong>. The first time, the PDF engine downloads once.</li>
    <li>Download the file, saved with <code>-pdfa</code> in its name.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If the file is rejected</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Check which version the portal wants. If it requires PDF/A-1 or PDF/A-3, ask whether PDF/A-2 is accepted.</li>
    <li>Damaged source files can fail validation. Try <a href="/repair-pdf">Repair PDF</a> first, then convert again.</li>
    <li>Remove passwords with <a href="/unlock-pdf">Unlock PDF</a> before converting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>Conversion runs in your browser with Ghostscript. Your document is never uploaded.</p>
</GuideLayout>
